@aston314 can you tell me more about what you mean by "Is it possible to customize the link for OpenAPI"?

You can use different OpenAI models, different models from other providers, self-host your own, or use ggml like @lstep mentioned. More information here: https://continue.dev/docs/customization#change-the-default-llm

You can also configure the settings for the OpenAI models and use our SDK to use different models when and where you want. If you share more on what you to do, we can give specific instructions on how to accomplish it

@irekgroup Since this comment 8 hours ago, I believe I have resolved this issue.

However, even if not, I've added the ability to view the server logs, which would expose the issue. You can see these by using the "Continue: View Logs" command in the VS Code command palette (cmd+shift+p).

If still not working, it would also be helpful to know operating system. Hoping to get you past this barrier asap—thanks!

@irekgroup The first error is known, and a solution was just released.

As for the latter, it might be possible that your OpenAI key doesn't have access to GPT-4. If you use "gpt-3.5-turbo" might this error disappear?

(you can edit this in config.py by changing the "default=..." line in "Models" to use model="gpt-3.5-turbo" instead of "gpt-4".)

@TKlerx , @vankeer I've since found the solution to the config not being acknowledged (errors were being swallowed, resulting in falling back to a default config), and the latest version on the VS Code Extension Marketplace will solve this.

It is true that the server runs after closing VS Code—that issue can be tracked here: #322 

And a couple of other notes, @vankeer: Since you are running the server manually, this will break the default config imports (they need an additional continuedev.src. appended in front), but you'll now see a traceback in the UI if anything is wrong with config.py. And it's a fair point to add a disclaimer. We're chipping away at these loading issues, but it might help set expectations.

@ferrantejake @vankeer An update for those watching this issue:

1. I'm working [here](https://github.com/continuedev/continue/issues/320) on a solution to bundle the python code as an executable, which I expect to solve this issue
2. For the time being, I've added documentation [here](https://github.com/continuedev/continue/tree/main/extension#troubleshooting) on how to troubleshoot this problem

@sestinj Thanks for your attention and commitment to making this work! I just tried the latest (apparently released only 30 mins ago!). It looks like the issue above is resolved! 

In testing the latest, I was still not able to get past starting the extension. I have made a ticket for it here: https://github.com/continuedev/continue/issues/345

I'll close this ticket since the root issue appears resolved.

@TKlerx It might seem slightly odd, but in this case the background process helps with speeding up loading time when reloading VS Code. This is also a very non-intensive process, so it has almost no penalty on the machine.

The server does not need to be restarted in order for the config file to be reloaded: this will be done automatically each time that the VS Code window is reloaded.

The last point is interesting and maybe cause for a new issue. We attempt to kill the server if it is running the old version, and if this is failing that would be a bug. Can you share what made you aware that the server version was old?

